    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ikuifip",
      "content": "Madhya Pradesh is a state in the Indian Union governed according to the provisions of the Constitution of India, particularly Part VI (Articles 152-237) which deals with the States. The state government follows the parliamentary form of government with a Governor as the Constitutional head and a Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister as the real executive."
    },
    {
      "id": "tqkktmj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.2 Governor of Madhya Pradesh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zwan471",
      "content": "The Governor is appointed by the President of India under Article 155 of the Constitution. The Governor holds office for a term of 5 years but is removable by the President at any time. The Governor must be a citizen of India and at least 35 years of age. The Governor acts as the constitutional head of the state and has several important functions and powers."
    },
    {
      "id": "3uxrxlc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Powers of the Governor"
    },
    {
      "id": "22fks7k",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Executive Powers - All executive actions of the state government are taken in the name of the Governor",
        "Legislative Powers - Summons, prorogues, and dissolves the State Legislature; addresses both Houses; gives assent to Bills",
        "Financial Powers - The Annual Budget is presented before the legislature with the recommendation of the Governor",
        "Judicial Powers - Appoints District Judges; High Court judges are appointed by the President in consultation with the Governor",
        "Discretionary Powers - Can exercise independent judgment in certain situations as per Article 163",
        "Pardoning Powers - Under Article 161, Governor can grant pardon, reprieve, respite, or remission of punishment"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n4776t4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.3 State Legislature of Madhya Pradesh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h57krbr",
      "content": "Madhya Pradesh has a unicameral legislature consisting of only one House - the Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly). The state does not have a Vidhan Parishad (Legislative Council). The Vidhan Sabha consists of 230 elected members. Elections are held every 5 years using the First-Past-the-Post (FPTP) electoral system."
    },
